\mode<article>{\subsection{Export, convert and verify}}

The author exported the live quiz from the web interface: a QTI archive
with 21 banks and 218 questions of five types --- single choice, multiple
answers, ordering, matching and fill in the blank.  An agent wrote a
converter to the JSON format of \texttt{canvaslms}, the command-line tool
we use for Canvas.  It then uploaded every bank to a sandbox course,
exported it again, and compared what came back with what went in, field by
field, with a checker written separately from the converter.  The checker
was itself tested by planting errors in converted banks (a mutation
test).  All 218 questions came back as they went in.

A converter that drops a field, or turns a code block's indentation into
something else, would otherwise have changed questions without anyone
noticing: the round trip through the real platform is what makes the
converted banks trustworthy enough to rewrite.

\mode<article>{\subsection{Decide how to draw}}

Canvas has no programming interface for creating New Quiz item banks, so
rewritten banks could not be put back as banks.  A Classic quiz with
question groups draws a question per student, but has no ordering
questions.  The author chose to draw per sitting: one question for each
position, the same for every student in that sitting, with the answer
options still shuffled per student.  Each draw leaves a record, so that a
student who retakes the exam never meets the same question again.

\mode<article>{\subsection{A style guide from the course itself}}

An item in a style the students never met tests the style, not the
objective.  One agent, with five reader agents, compiled how the course
material writes Python --- docstrings, naming, f-strings, [[with open]],
what is and is not taught --- with a file and line number as evidence for
every rule.  The author pointed to a second source: every run of code in
every lecture and tutorial (\foreignlanguage{swedish}{\emph{övning}}) is
recorded in a learnlog repository.  A second
agent added that evidence: 18 sessions, about 900 runs, 33 sessions in the
interactive interpreter.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{A style guide from the course itself}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Course material: one agent with five readers, every rule with
        a file and line number.
      \item Live coding: 18 recorded sessions, about 900 runs.
      \item Where the course was inconsistent, the author decided:
        English identifiers; one-line Swedish docstrings; getters,
        \texttt{@property} and public attributes all allowed; only taught
        constructs.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

Where the course was inconsistent, the author decided.  Identifiers are in
English; docstrings are one line of Swedish naming the parameters and the
return value; all three ways to expose an attribute --- getters,
[[@property]] and public attributes --- are allowed, since the students
saw all three; only constructs the course teaches appear, so negative
indices are allowed, because they were explained live, and so is
[[f"{x=}"]], explained in class though the recordings could not show it.

The style guide has a second use, which is about cues.  A distractor may
break the course's style only in the aspect the item tests.  Otherwise the
one option written as the course writes it is the key, and a student who
has merely seen the course's code can find it.

\mode<article>{\subsection{Seven rewriters}}

Seven agents rewrote the banks in parallel, one per learning objective and
three banks each, all from one written brief.  Its rules turn the checks
of \cref{sec:items} into requirements.

Every program and every option was run.  Every ordering item was tried in
every order of its lines and every indentation, so that exactly one
works.  No option refers to other options, since the answers are shuffled.
Scoring is all or nothing, as the digits require, and each bank keeps at
least eight items.  Every change is logged with its evidence, and every
bank was uploaded to the sandbox and verified by exporting it again.

\mode<article>{\subsection{Trying every order}\label{sec:checker}}

\leavevmode\ltnote{%
  The checker is developed here, after its run was shown with
  \cref{ex:item-order}, so that the program answers a question the reader
  already has: how was the second order found?  The slides show only what
  it does and its result; the code is for the notes, where it can be read
  in full.
}%
The second order of Item~2 was found by a program.  Testing every
order is what Ihantola and Karavirta proposed for Parsons problems: an
environment \textcquote[IIP-127]{ihantola2011}{that tests all the possible
combinations, alerts the author about alternative solutions, and leaves it
for the author to decide which of the solutions should be accepted}.  The
idea is brute force: an ordering item has few lines, five lines have
$5! = 120$ orders, and running 120 small programs takes a fraction of a
second.  We give the program's layout first and refine its parts as the
reasoning needs them.

\mode<presentation>{%
  \begin{frame}
    \begin{block}{\texttt{parsons.py}: trying every order}
      The checker behind Item~2:
      \begin{itemize}
        \item builds the program of each of the $5! = 120$ orders of the
          lines;
        \item runs each with \texttt{exec} in a fresh namespace and
          catches what it prints;
        \item keeps the orders that print what the key prints, after
          checking that the key itself runs.
      \end{itemize}
      Result: 2 of 120 orders print \texttt{27}.  The code is in the
      notes.
    \end{block}
  \end{frame}
}

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{block}{\texttt{parsons.py}}
    \mode<presentation>{\setminted{fontsize=\scriptsize}}%
    The layout of the checker:
<<[[parsons.py]]>>=
"""Find the orders of a Parsons problem's lines that work"""

<<checker's imports>>

<<the item's lines and key>>


<<checker's helper functions>>


def main():
    """Print every order that prints what the key prints"""
    <<run the key to see what a working order prints>>
    <<try every order, keep those that print the same>>
    <<print the working orders>>


main()
@
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

The item is its lines, in the order the student sees them, and its key,
the order the item's author accepted, given as positions in that list.

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{block}{The item}
    The lines of Item~2 and its key:
<<the item's lines and key>>=
LINES = [
    "print(total)",
    "for number in numbers:",
    "total = 0",
    "    total += number",
    "numbers = [4, 8, 15]",
]
KEY = (2, 4, 1, 3, 0)
@
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

An order is a tuple of positions, and the program it gives is those lines
joined.  The indentation is part of each line here, as the student sees
it; the rewriters' checkers also tried every indentation, for items whose
lines are shown flush left.

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{block}{From an order to a program}
    An order picks the lines:
<<checker's helper functions>>=
def program(order):
    """Return the program with LINES in the given order"""
    return "\n".join(LINES[index] for index in order)


@
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

To compare an order with the key we need what each program prints.  We
run the program inside the checker with [[exec]] and catch what it prints
with [[redirect_stdout]].  Most orders fail: a [[for]] without a body is
an [[IndentationError]], a variable used before its assignment a
[[NameError]].  A failing order is simply not a solution, so any exception
gives [[None]]; [[SyntaxError]] and [[IndentationError]] are exceptions
like the others.  Every run gets a fresh, empty namespace ([[{}]]), so an
order cannot borrow a variable that an earlier order defined.

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{block}{What a program prints}
    Running a program inside the checker:
<<checker's helper functions>>=
def output(source):
    """Return what source prints, or None if it fails"""
    printed = io.StringIO()
    try:
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(printed):
            exec(source, {})
    except Exception:
        return None
    return printed.getvalue()
<<checker's imports>>=
import contextlib
import io
@
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

This checker needs no time limit, because its only loop runs over a fixed
list; an item with a [[while]] loop needs one, since some order may loop
forever.  Nor does it need more than one run per order, because the
program reads no input.  For a program that reads input, every order must
be tried on several inputs: an order can agree with the key on one input
and not on another.

The key's output is the standard.  The key is not trusted to be the only
working order --- that is the point of the checker --- but it must work,
or every failing order would match its failure.  That is Item~1's flaw
again, and the checker reports it rather than comparing against it.

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{block}{The standard to compare with}
    The key must work:
<<run the key to see what a working order prints>>=
expected = output(program(KEY))
if expected is None:
    print("The key itself fails.")
    return
@
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

Then every order is run and compared with the key.  [[permutations]]
gives all $5! = 120$ orders of the five positions.

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{block}{Every order}
    Try each order and keep those that print what the key prints:
<<try every order, keep those that print the same>>=
orders = list(itertools.permutations(range(len(LINES))))
working = []
for order in orders:
    if output(program(order)) == expected:
        working.append(order)
<<checker's imports>>=
import itertools
@
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

Last, the program prints how many orders work, and each of them.  A sound
item gives one; Item~2 gives two.

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{block}{The report}
    Print the working orders:
<<print the working orders>>=
print(f"{len(working)} of {len(orders)} orders print {expected}")
for order in working:
    print(program(order))
    print()
@
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{example}[The whole checker (\texttt{parsons.py})]
    \label{ex:checker-whole}
    Putting each part in place of its name gives the whole program:
    \inputminted{python}{examples/parsons.py}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

Its run is the one shown with Item~2.

\mode<article>{\subsection{Three independent reviewers}}

Three further agents reviewed the rewritten banks, two or three objectives
each.  They ran everything with harnesses of their own, read the
rewriters' change logs only after forming their own view, and looked for
cues (length, grammar, patterns), wrong keys and personal data.  They
found no blockers, six major defects and about 40 minor ones; the original
rewriters fixed all of them in one round.

\mode<article>{\subsection{Feedback on every option}}

Every item got feedback, modelled on the practice exam written the same
week (\cref{sec:rest}): a neutral text, a text for a right answer, one
for a wrong answer, and one text per option, 588 option texts in all.
The neutral text has three parts, in Swedish:
\foreignlanguage{swedish}{\enquote{Tänk på}} (what to think about),
\foreignlanguage{swedish}{\enquote{Prova}} (how to check it in the Python
terminal) and \foreignlanguage{swedish}{\enquote{I kursen}}
(where in the course to study).  Every pointer into the course was checked
against a real heading or a recorded live-coding session, and every claim
about code was run.  A wrong-answer text never names the key.

\Cref{sec:feedback-example} shows the feedback of an invented item as a
student sees it.  Feedback of this kind, which explains and points to
what to study, is what meta-analyses of feedback on computer-based items
find most effective on average: clearly more than feedback that only
says right or wrong, and somewhat more than showing the correct
answer.\autocite{vanderkleij2015,mertens2022} The margin over the correct
answer is small, though, and one analysis found none for multiple-choice
items;\autocite[1764]{mertens2022} and feedback does not always help: in
about a third of the studies in two large meta-analyses it lowered
performance.\autocite[156]{shute2008}\footnote{Students also skip
explanations, more so when the feedback comes late or when they have
already been shown the correct
answer.\autocite{vanderkleij2012,manez2020} The scientific basis is in
\cref{app:feedback}, which asks whether feedback that explains helps more
than right or wrong, and when it does not.}  That students who had been
shown the correct answer seldom opened the explanation\autocite[65]{%
manez2020} is one reason to keep the key out of the wrong-answer texts,
though not a measured gain in learning.  Feedback that a student reads
only after an exam is delayed feedback, where the evidence is mixed; a
practice exam, with its feedback right after each attempt, is where such
texts are best supported.

\mode<article>{\subsection{A literate drawing program}}

The draw is done by a program, \texttt{dra.nw}, a literate program in
Python with Swedish prose, which entered the repository through a pull
request.  The draw is seeded and reproducible, and every sitting leaves a
record: the seed, checksums of every input, the version of Python.  An
option ([[--undvik]], \enquote{avoid}) takes earlier records, so a student
who retakes the exam never meets the same question twice.  The program
refuses to write a file that git would track, since the repository is
public and the drawn exam is exam content, and it never writes to Canvas.
An independent agent reviewed it twice; it has 38 tests, and every bug we
planted in it (every mutant) made a test fail.  \Cref{sec:draw-example}
runs it on an invented exam.

\mode<article>{\subsection{An example exam}}

Finally an example exam was drawn and created, unpublished, in the
sandbox course for the author to read: 21 items, full marks 3\,333\,333,
every item checked against the draw after an export.

\mode<article>{\subsection{Who did what}}

About 20 agents took part: one converter, one style agent with five
readers, one for the live coding, seven rewriters, three reviewers, one
author and one reviewer of the drawing program.  Most ran on the most
capable model available.  The orchestrator --- the agent session the
author talked to --- held the decisions and checked every agent's result
itself: leak scans, counts, consistency across banks.  We did not measure
the cost.

\begin{frame}
