\mode<article>{\subsection{A rewrite, before and after}%
\label{sec:rewrite-example}}

The \enquote{before} is written in the style of the old banks:
Swedish identifiers, no docstrings, a string built with [[+]] and
[[str]], and options among which the key is the only full sentence and the
longest.  The \enquote{after} follows the rules of the course's style
guide: English identifiers, a one-line Swedish docstring that names the
parameter and says what is returned, an f-string, formatted by black,
and options of one form, none of them longer than the key.  It stays a
short fragment to trace, without [[main]], which the style guide allows
for such items.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[An invented item, before and after]
    \label{ex:rewrite}
    \mode<presentation>{\setminted{fontsize=\tiny}\scriptsize}%
    Both versions ask \enquote{Vilken utskrift ger programmet?}
    \begin{columns}[T]
    \begin{column}{0.47\linewidth}
    Before:
<<[[before.py]]>>=
def medel(siffror):
    summa = 0
    for s in siffror:
        summa = summa + s
    return summa / len(siffror)


betyg = [4, 5, 3]
print("Medel: " + str(medel(betyg)))
@
    Options: \texttt{4}; \texttt{12}; \enquote{Ett fel}; key
    \enquote{Medel: 4.0}.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.51\linewidth}
    After:
<<[[after.py]]>>=
def mean(grades):
    """Returnerar medelvärdet av betygen grades."""
    total = 0
    for grade in grades:
        total += grade
    return total / len(grades)


grades = [4, 5, 3]
print(f"Medelvärdet är {mean(grades)}.")
@
    Options: \enquote{Medelvärdet är 4.}; key \enquote{Medelvärdet är
    4.0.}; \enquote{Medelvärdet är 12.0.}; \enquote{Medelvärdet är
    1.0.}
    \end{column}
    \end{columns}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

The two programs compute the same thing, and running them shows it; the
output differs only in its wording, since the course prints whole
sentences that end with a full stop.

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{example}[Both versions run]
    The old version:
    \runpython[showcommand]{examples/before.py}
    and the new:
    \runpython[showcommand]{examples/after.py}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

The options changed more than the code.  In the old version a student
who only knows that a program prints sentences picks the key; in the new
one every option is a sentence, the key is not the longest, and each
distractor is what a particular misconception gives: integer division
gives \texttt{4}, forgetting to divide gives \texttt{12.0}, and keeping
only the last grade gives \texttt{1.0}.

