\mode<article>{\subsection{Measuring a cue}\label{sec:guess-example}}

The reviewers measured cues by playing the test-wise student: a guessing
rule is run against every item of a bank, and the number of keys it finds
is compared with what random guessing finds.  We wrote some of the
invented items above with the old banks' cue, the key as the only long
option, to see whether the measurement shows it.  The program takes the
banks on its command line.

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{example}[\texttt{guess.py}]
    The layout of the program:
<<[[guess.py]]>>=
"""Count how often simple guessing rules find the key in a bank"""

import html
import json
import re
import sys


<<guessing rules>>


<<reading the items of a bank>>


def main():
    """Print how many keys each rule finds in the banks given"""
    items = []
    for path in sys.argv[1:]:
        items.extend(bank_items(path))
    <<print what each rule finds>>
    <<print what random guessing finds>>


main()
@
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

A rule sees only the options, never the stem, and returns the position
of the option it would tick.  We give it four rules: the longest option,
the shortest, the first option of 30 characters or more, and always the
third, a rule that knows nothing at all.

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{example}[The rules]
    Each rule picks an option by its form alone:
<<guessing rules>>=
def longest(options):
    """Return the position of the longest option, the first if tied"""
    best = 0
    for index, option in enumerate(options):
        if len(option) > len(options[best]):
            best = index
    return best


def shortest(options):
    """Return the position of the shortest option, the first if tied"""
    best = 0
    for index, option in enumerate(options):
        if len(option) < len(options[best]):
            best = index
    return best


def first_long(options):
    """Return the position of the first option of LONG characters or more"""
    for index, option in enumerate(options):
        if len(option) >= LONG:
            return index
    return 0


def third(options):
    """Return the position of the third option, whatever it says"""
    return 2


LONG = 30
RULES = [
    ("pick the longest", longest),
    ("pick the shortest", shortest),
    (f"pick the first of {LONG}+ characters", first_long),
    ("always pick the third", third),
]
@
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

The items are on the New Quizzes format, as in the banks: the options are
HTML fragments, and the key is the identifier of one of them.

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{example}[Reading a bank]
    The options as plain text, and the position of the key:
<<reading the items of a bank>>=
def plain(fragment):
    """Return the text of an HTML fragment, without its tags"""
    return html.unescape(re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", "", fragment))


def bank_items(path):
    """Return (options, position of the key) for each item of a bank"""
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as file:
        bank = json.load(file)
    items = []
    for item in bank["items"]:
        entry = item["entry"]
        choices = entry["interaction_data"]["choices"]
        options = [plain(choice["item_body"]) for choice in choices]
        ids = [choice["id"] for choice in choices]
        items.append((options, ids.index(entry["scoring_data"]["value"])))
    return items
@
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

Then each rule is run against every item, and random guessing gives the
standard: one over the number of options, summed over the items.

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{example}[The table]
    What each rule finds, and what chance finds:
<<print what each rule finds>>=
print(f"{'Rule':<36}Keys found")
for name, rule in RULES:
    found = 0
    for options, key in items:
        if rule(options) == key:
            found += 1
    print(f"{name:<36}{found} of {len(items)}")
<<print what random guessing finds>>=
chance = 0
for options, key in items:
    chance += 1 / len(options)
label = "guessing at random (expected)"
print(f"{label:<36}{chance:.1f} of {len(items)}")
@
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[Measuring a cue in the invented banks]
    \label{ex:guess}
    Four guessing rules against the 12 invented items:
    \runpython[showcommand, breaklines,
      args={draw/banks/1.1.json draw/banks/1.2.json draw/banks/1.3.json}]{%
      examples/guess.py}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

Ticking the longest option finds five keys of 12, against three for
random guessing; the rule of 30 characters finds the same five and one
more by its fallback, the first option.  The cue we planted is there to
measure.  In the bank that a rewriter tested
with a dozen rules, the best rule found two keys of nine
(\cref{sec:lessons}).

