\leavevmode\ltnote{%
  The defects are grouped by the check that found them, not by bank or
  by type, so that the four checks of \cref{sec:items} return as the
  structure of the results: the same dimension, now with the real
  values.
}%
The defects in the live banks, each confirmed by running code, fall into
the classes of \cref{sec:items} and a few more.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Found by running}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Keys that crash: the caller's variables used
        (\texttt{NameError}).
      \item A \enquote{not found} message never printed: a flag set
        outside its \texttt{if}.
      \item Wrong keys: a loop the key said gives 28 gives 21; two
        items with no correct option.
      \item Options that can never work: an \texttt{IndentationError},
        arguments \texttt{print} does not have.
      \item \texttt{except} for errors never raised at run time
        (\texttt{SyntaxError}).
      \item A harmful fix as key: catching \texttt{TypeError} to hide a
        missing \texttt{int()}.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

The last of these is worth a moment.  The key did make the program
survive bad input --- by catching the exception that the missing
conversion caused, so that the program then rejected even valid
input.  An
item that rewards that fix teaches it.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Found by trying orders, measuring, reading the bank}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Ordering items with two valid orders, or none (a stray
        apostrophe, a double space).
      \item The key the longest option in nine of 11 and eight of 10 items
        of two banks; the same answer pattern in six of seven items of another.
      \item Exact duplicates, six in one bank alone; items in the wrong
        bank.
      \item A note to a colleague, visible to students; real staff names
        and e-mail addresses in one item.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

The reviews of the rewritten banks found no blocker.  The six majors were
cues (option length, and a near-identical pair of options that always
contained the key), an ambiguity that a student running the code would
hit, a made-up personal identity number (\foreignlanguage{swedish}{%
personnummer}), and a fill-in item that tested the wrong objective.  None
was a wrong key.


\section{Lessons}
\label{sec:lessons}

The lessons are this case's experience: what happened here, in one
course and one session, not general findings.  The first is the one the
results already show.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Run it, don't read it}
    Every key, every distractor and every claim in the feedback was
    executed.  That is how the crashing keys, the wrong keys and the
    options that can never work were found.
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

Every defect found by running sat in an item that someone had written
and read, meaning the program to work.  Running it costs a second.

The next lesson concerns the orchestrator itself.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{exercise}\label{ex:orchestrator}
    Two bugs had been reported in the banks.  The orchestrator ran the
    code of both questions, found it correct, and told the author that the
    reports were false.  They were true.  How could that happen?
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

\leavevmode\ltnote{%
  The orchestrator's mistake is told as a try-first question
  (\cref{ex:orchestrator}) because a check that ran code reads as final;
  guessing the cause lets the colleagues find for themselves that the
  evidence came from the wrong version of the bank.
}%
The orchestrator had run the code --- of the wrong version.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{solution}[A claim is a hypothesis, including your own]
    The orchestrator had run an older export, from May 2025, in which the
    code was still correct; the bank had been edited since.  A rewriter,
    who matched the questions by their identifiers and ran the live
    export, showed that both bugs were real.
  \end{solution}
  \begin{block}{Numbering lies; identifiers don't}
    A report numbered the questions after a January export; the November
    export ordered them differently.  Match by identifier.
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

What caught the mistake was not more care by the orchestrator but a second
agent that checked the same claim independently, from the live data.  A
related mistake came from the numbering: a comparison report numbered the
questions after an export from January, and the November export ordered
them differently, so several claims that looked wrong were right claims
about another question.

In this case the rewriters checked their own work thoroughly, and the
reviewers still found six majors: cues, wording, a made-up personal
identity number and an item that tested the wrong objective.  None was a
wrong key.  Here, at least, the defects that got past the authors' own
checks were of the kinds that running the code does not show.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Independent review found more}
    The rewriters ran everything; the reviewers still found six majors:
    cues, wording, a made-up personal identity number, an item testing
    the wrong objective.  None was a wrong key.
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

Cues are a design problem, not a typo, and they can be measured.  One
rewriter tested a dozen guessing rules, of the kind a test-wise student
uses, against a bank; the best of them solved two items of nine.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Cues are a design problem, not a typo}
    The longest option; the only option in course style; a near-identical
    pair; grammar; \enquote{none of the above} under shuffling.  Measure
    them: a dozen guessing rules against one bank; the best solved two
    items of nine.
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

The tools failed silently more than once.  [[canvaslms quizzes create]]
exits with status 0 even when Canvas rejected an item, saying
\enquote{N failed} only on its last line.  Canvas rejects a
multiple-answer item with one particular property.  And KTH's web firewall
answers 403 to a fill-in item whose regular expression alternates between
two kinds of quotes.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Silent failures in the tools}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item \texttt{quizzes create} exits 0 when Canvas rejected an item.
      \item Canvas rejects a multiple-answer item with one particular
        property.
      \item The web firewall answers 403 to a fill-in pattern that
        alternates between two kinds of quotes.
    \end{itemize}
    Read the output, and verify by export.
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

The course repository is public, so exam content never entered it: the
banks live in a folder git ignores, every diff was scanned for any text
from the banks before it was committed, and the drawing program refuses to
write where git would track.  One review still found a made-up personal
identity number and real-looking user names in the banks themselves.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Privacy and a public repository}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Exam content in a folder git ignores.
      \item Every diff scanned against all the text in the banks.
      \item The drawing program refuses to write where git would track.
      \item A review still found a made-up personal identity number and
        real-looking user names in the banks.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

Ten points of style were genuinely open in the course material.  Each went
to the author with its evidence and a recommendation; the remaining
questions followed the recommendation and were logged.

Last, a small tool earned a literate program and a review.  The reviews
of the drawing program found real bugs: a record could be overwritten,
outputs could land where git tracks them, and a quiz file could be written
without its record.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{The author decides; the agents prepare the decision}
    Ten open points of style went to the author with evidence and a
    recommendation.  The rest followed the recommendation and were
    logged.
  \end{block}
  \begin{block}{Literate and reviewed, even for a small tool}
    The reviews of the drawing program found real bugs: a record could
    be overwritten, outputs could land where git tracks them, a quiz file
    could be written without its record.
  \end{block}
\end{frame}


\section[The rest]{The rest of the week}
\label{sec:rest}

The banks were one part of the session.  The others prepared the sitting
itself.  The order of the points had been reversed in January 2026, so
that objective~1 no longer sat in the units; we found the commit, restored
the order $10^{L-1}$ and fixed the grading script, through pull requests
with independent reviews.  The exam's IP ranges were set from the booked
rooms (with \texttt{kthutils iprange}), and students with extra time from
Funka got it (codes R0, +50\,\%, and R1, +25\,\%) through
\texttt{canvaslms}.  Both were then automated in a \texttt{setup} command
of the grading script, which reads the schedule, the rooms, the registered
students and the Funka lists, and verifies what Canvas saved.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{The rest of the week}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Points order: restored $10^{L-1}$ (reversed in January) and
        fixed the grading script.
      \item IP ranges from the booked rooms; extra time for Funka (R0
        +50\,\%, R1 +25\,\%).
      \item Both automated in a \texttt{setup} command that verifies what
        Canvas saved.
      \item An independent practice exam, written without the banks.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

One agent wrote an independent exam from the course material alone,
without ever seeing the banks, and a second compared it with them.  It
became a practice exam, \foreignlanguage{swedish}{\emph{Exempelprov 2}},
with feedback on every option, placed unpublished in the course for the
author's review.  Its feedback was the model for the feedback in the
banks.


\section[Your course]{Doing this in your own course}
\label{sec:own-course}

\leavevmode\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{RapportLOAdapt}.

  The report ends on what the colleague would do, sorted into prepare,
  decide and never delegate, so that the lessons become a plan.  The
  last list is the one that matters most and is kept shortest.
}%
If you want to do this in a course of your own, our experience from
this one case comes down to three lists: what we needed to have prepared
before any agent started, what the author decided as the work went, and
what we would never delegate.  They are what worked here, not a tested
recipe.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Prepare}
    The course material where an agent can read it (live coding too); an
    export of the banks and a converter verified by a round trip; a
    sandbox course; a place outside the repository for exam content, and
    a leak scan for every commit.
  \end{block}
  \begin{block}{Decide}
    Open points of style; how to draw and avoid repeats; scoring; what
    the feedback says.
  \end{block}
  \begin{block}{Never delegate}
    The decisions; the privacy rules; the final read of the exam.
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

In this case the preparation is what let the agents check rather than
guess: the course material as evidence of style, the platform as the test
of a converted or rewritten bank, and a place for exam content that the
repository never sees.

Here the decisions were the author's, and an agent prepared each of
them with evidence and a recommendation.

The agents did the work and checked it thoroughly; that did not make
them responsible for the exam.  The author made the decisions and set the
privacy rules, and the example exam was created for the author to read
before the sitting.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{summary}
    In short:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item A defective item decides a digit for the students who draw it.
      \item Run it, try every order, measure the bank, see it as a
        student does.
      \item Here, agents prepared and verified, independent agents
        checked them, and the author decided.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{summary}
\end{frame}
